## Probing Transformer Dynamics With Greek Scrambling: Resolving\
\
## Syntactic Transformation Under Semantic Equivalence\
\
```\
ACL Submitted: December 21, 2025\
```\
## Summary\
\
```\
The paper probes how syntactic word-order information (SVO vs. VSO) unfolds across layers\
of a Greek-tuned transformer (Llama-Krikri-8B-Instruct) using tightly controlled object-relative\
minimal pairs that preserve meaning while varying only order. Linear probes trained on simple\
distributional summaries of hidden states show that sentence-type information is not decodable\
before the clause boundary, peaks in middle layers with broad cross-layer generalization, and\
becomes less linearly decodable in late layers; decision boundaries learned on late layers do\
not transfer to earlier layers. The authors interpret this as evidence for a mid-layer \'93structural\
zone\'94 and a late \'93semanticization,\'94 offering hypotheses about staged, interactive computation\
that can be linked to human neurocognitive timing.\
```\
## Strengths\
\
```\
Technical novelty and innovation\
The use of Greek scrambling minimal pairs to keep meaning constant while varying\
word order is well-motivated and typologically informative for probing syntax\'96\
semantics dissociation.\
Anchoring analyses to a clause-initial complementizer and explicitly contrasting pre-\
vs. post-clause regions is a clear design to localize when structure becomes\
decodable.\
Experimental rigor and validation\
Layer-wise and cross-layer generalization analyses, including cluster-based\
permutation statistics, provide a coherent picture of mid-layer concentration and\
late-layer reorganization.\
The late-to-early generalization failure adds a useful negative-transfer diagnostic,\
complementing the cross-layer generalization matrix.\
```\
\
```\
Clarity of presentation\
The high-level motivation, relation to neurocognitive components (ELAN/LAN,\
N400/P600), and the staged/interactive interpretation are articulated clearly and\
cautiously framed as hypotheses rather than direct mappings.\
Methods are generally described in sufficient detail to understand how features and\
probes were constructed.\
Significance of contributions\
The study adds a language- and phenomenon-specific case (Greek scrambling) to\
a growing body of work showing mid-layer concentration of structural information,\
thereby enriching cross-linguistic evidence.\
It contributes to the debate on serial vs. interactive processing in models and\
suggests concrete follow-ups for causal testing (e.g., activation patching in the\
\'93structural zone\'94).\
```\
## Weaknesses\
\
```\
Technical limitations or concerns\
The probing approach reduces each sentence-layer region to four global moments\
(mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis) of flattened activations, discarding token and\
positional structure; achieving very high AUC (~0.9) from such coarse summaries\
raises concerns about confounds or probe artifacts.\
Only a single instruction-tuned model is studied; instruction tuning and bilingual\
training may bias representational dynamics, and no base-model or multi-model\
replication is provided.\
Experimental gaps or methodological issues\
Dataset size is small (128 sentences across 32 conditions), and 10-fold CV over\
such a small pool risks instability; significance testing over folds rather than items is\
a weak unit of analysis.\
There are no probe selectivity controls (e.g., random-label controls, control tasks\
per Hewitt & Liang/Pimentel et al.), no untrained-model baseline, and no checks for\
length/tokenization imbalances or surface heuristics.\
The claim that pre-clause decoding is at chance is plausible, but the inclusion of the\
complementizer in the \'93before\'94 region is a design choice that should be justified\
further; more granular token- or position-level analyses would strengthen the\
localization claim.\
Clarity or presentation issues\
Some internal inconsistencies (e.g., \'93These layer-by-position activations constitute\
the sole inputs\'94 vs. global moment summaries; references to \'93Appendix ??\'94;\
placeholder table artifacts) detract from polish.\
Details on the exact layer count and model configuration (e.g., confirming 32\
transformer blocks; attention/MLP specs) could be clarified for reproducibility.\
```\
\
```\
Missing related work or comparisons\
The work would benefit from engaging more directly with the probing literature on\
layerwise grammatical decodability and probe selectivity (e.g., decoding probing;\
control tasks; information-theoretic probing).\
Comparisons to other word-order-flexible settings and benchmarks (e.g., TurBLiMP;\
Spanish word-order generation-order work) and to recent layer\'96EEG dynamics\
papers would situate the findings more firmly and calibrate novelty.\
```\
## Detailed Comments\
\
```\
Technical soundness evaluation\
The core design\'97minimal pairs in Greek that vary order under semantic\
equivalence\'97is strong for isolating syntactic form. The clause boundary anchoring\
is sensible and likely drives the clean pre-/post-clause contrast.\
However, the probe aggregates rich, structured hidden-state tensors into four global\
moments. Such aggressive compression can conflate multiple factors (e.g., token\
count, position-specific activation magnitudes, frequency- or tokenization-driven\
shifts) and makes interpretation difficult: why should a global mean or kurtosis\
scalar track a structural label so strongly? Without selectivity controls (randomized\
labels, matched-length controls, unsupervised subspace baselines), it is hard to\
rule out degenerate cues.\
Statistical inference uses cluster-based permutation over folds rather than items.\
Since folds share the same items across layers, the independence assumptions are\
unclear. A more appropriate item-level bootstrap or hierarchical permutation (over\
items and layers) is recommended.\
Experimental evaluation assessment\
Single-model results limit generalizability. Prior work shows mid-layer peaks for\
syntax in many models (e.g., decoding probing on BLiMP; speech-model minimal\
pairs; semantic anomaly detection mid\'96late), so replication on a base LLaMA-3.1-\
8B, a non-instruction-tuned Greek model (if available), and at least one multilingual\
baseline would strengthen claims.\
The very small dataset (128 sentences) and strong reported AUC invite concerns\
about overfitting or spurious correlations. Reporting per-condition performance,\
length/subword-count parity, and tokenization diagnostics would help.\
Additional probes (simple MLP; linear probes on token- or position-specific\
summaries; RSA) could cross-validate the main effect and test whether\
\'93semanticization\'94 is a linear separability loss or a geometric re-embedding.\
```\
\
```\
Comparison with related work (using the summaries provided)\
He et al. (decoding probing, 2403.17299) and He et al. (speech minimal pairs,\
2509.15655) both find linguistic decodability peaking in lower\'96middle to middle\
layers with late degradation for some objectives, consistent with the mid-layer\
\'93structural zone\'94 reported here. Positioning your results as a typological extension\
(Greek scrambling) would be accurate and strengthen claims by citing these\
precedents.\
Zacharopoulos & Kyriakoglou (2511.19232) show semantic anomaly detection in\
mid\'96late layers with earlier geometry changes; your findings complement theirs by\
focusing on syntactic order under semantic invariance. A joint discussion could\
clarify how \'93semantic anomaly\'94 vs. \'93syntactic order\'94 are temporally distributed\
across layers.\
The Spanish generation-order analysis (2502.14451) highlights causal-model\
biases toward SVO and suggests that decoder-only objectives may narrow surface\
variability; discussing whether Llama-Krikri exhibits an English/SVO bias would\
contextualize your late-layer \'93semanticization\'94 claim.\
TurBLiMP (2506.13487) demonstrates that flexible word order and subordination\
stress test models; linking your controlled Greek paradigm to broader typological\
benchmarks would increase relevance and motivate future cross-lingual tests.\
The large-scale brain\'96model dynamics study (2509.24307) finds middle-to-high-\
layer alignment with N400-like timing; while your mapping from layers to EEG\
components is necessarily indirect, acknowledging these dynamics and proposing\
concrete co-analyses (e.g., vRSA time-by-layer alignment) would enhance the\
neurocognitive claims.\
Discussion of broader impact and significance\
The study advances a principled approach to isolating syntactic information and\
suggests a credible layerwise computation story: early emergence post-cue, mid-\
layer stabilization, late reorganization. This aligns with the broader literature and\
can guide causal mechanistic experiments (activation patching, head ablations)\
inside the identified \'93structural zone.\'94\
The main risk is over-interpretation: given the coarse probing features and limited\
dataset, the strength of the neurocognitive mapping should be described as\
suggestive. Strengthening selectivity and cross-model replication will make the\
proposed staged/interactive account more compelling and robust.\
```\
## Questions for Authors\
\
1. How do you ensure that the high AUC from four global moments is not driven by trivial\
    confounds (e.g., subword-length differences, position-specific magnitude shifts, or\
    residual tokenization imbalances)? Can you report length/subword parity and a random-\
    label control AUC?\
\
\
2. Why aggregate to global moment features instead of position-aware or token-level\
    summaries? Do token-specific linear probes (e.g., on verb vs. noun positions) yield\
    consistent mid-layer peaks?\
3. Can you include selectivity controls (e.g., control tasks following Hewitt & Liang; an\
    untrained or randomly reinitialized model baseline; a base LLaMA-3.1-8B probe) to\
    assess whether the probe exploits true structure rather than superficial cues?\
4. How stable are the findings across larger datasets (beyond 128 items) and across model\
    variants (base vs. instruction-tuned, monolingual vs. multilingual)? Any preliminary\
    evidence?\
5. The \'93before\'94 region includes the complementizer. Would excluding the complementizer or\
    probing at finer temporal slices (per token or sliding windows) change the locus and\
    onset of decodability?\
6. Can you quantify the \'93semanticization\'94 claim with geometry metrics (e.g., RSA, CKA with\
    semantic features, participation ratio) to show that linear separability decreases while\
    other structure persists in late layers?\
7. Do late-to-early negative transfer results persist when using nonlinear probes or when\
    conditioning on balanced tokenization/length?\
\
## Overall Assessment\
\
This is a timely and well-motivated study that leverages a clever Greek scrambling paradigm to\
cleanly probe syntactic structure formation in a strong Greek-capable LLM. The main empirical\
patterns\'97no decodability pre-clause, mid-layer concentration with broad cross-layer\
generalization, and late-layer reorganization\'97are consistent with and add typological breadth\
to prior layerwise probing literature. However, key methodological choices (extreme\
compression to four global moments, small dataset, lack of selectivity controls and multi-model\
replication) limit the strength of the causal and mechanistic claims. The paper is promising and\
conceptually valuable, but to meet ACL\'92s standards I recommend revision to include stronger\
controls, broader replication, more granular analyses, and clearer statistical procedures. With\
these improvements, the work could make a solid, well-supported contribution to the discourse\
on staged and interactive computation in LLMs and its relation to human processing.\
\
\
}